/E (In time-based Dollar Cost Averaging, investors follow a consistent schedule for investing a fixed amount of money at regular time intervals, irrespective of the asset's price. This can be a small amount, such as $50 or $100, or a larger amount, depending on the investment goals. This method relies on the passage of time, and investments are made based on a predetermined schedule, such as daily, weekly, monthly, or quarterly. Time-based DCA is designed to minimize the impact of market timing, allowing investors to benefit from the natural fluctuations of the market over a set period to ultimately enter at an optimal price. This is a long-term investment strategy rather than trading.)
/Pg 137 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 893 0 R
/T (Level Based Dollar Cost Averaging)
/E (Level Based Dollar Cost Averaging)
/Pg 137 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 893 0 R
/E (Contrastingly, level-based Dollar Cost Averaging involves scaling into trade positions based on the asset's price levels. Instead of following a fixed schedule, traders monitor the market and make purchases when the asset's price reaches certain predefined levels. For example, if the price drops below a specified threshold, the trader may decide to buy more units. This approach is more reactive to market conditions and involves adjusting investment levels based on the perceived value or attractiveness of the asset at a given time. Additionally, it allows traders to manage risk as they can scale into positions at different price points, providing a better average entry.)
/Pg 137 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
